.TH "tum\-import" "1" "" "" "tmux\-borrow Manual"
.SS NAME
\f[B]tum\-import\f[R] \[em] adopt the current pane as a utility
(anonymous or named)
.SS SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tum import\f[R] [\f[I]name\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]tum import\f[R] \f[B]\-h\f[R] | \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
Adopt the current tmux pane as a managed utility.
With no name, the import is \f[B]anonymous\f[R]: the pane is tagged
\f[CR]src=imported\-anon\f[R], given an auto\-generated utility name,
and becomes borrowable, hideable, and closeable like any utility.
No file is written; close it and it is gone, and it cannot survive a
server restart.
.PP
With a \f[I]name\f[R], the import is \f[B]named\f[R]: command,
arguments, working directory, and environment are captured at maximum
fidelity from \f[CR]/proc/$pid/\f[R] and written to a
\f[CR]<name>.util\f[R] definition file (\f[CR]src=imported\-named\f[R]).
Thereafter it is a normal, editable utility, available in any session.
The captured file is meant to be hand\-edited to refine.
.PP
Named import needs Linux for \f[CR]/proc/$pid/\f[R] capture; anonymous
import and every other command work anywhere tmux does.
PID is read ephemerally at import time only \[em] it is never stored as
a binding key.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R] Print a short synopsis (the SYNOPSIS
and EXAMPLES sections of this page) and exit.
For bare \f[B]tum\f[R], list every subcommand.
.SS EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
tum import               # anonymous: temporary, not saved
tum import prod\-db       # named: captures cmd/args/cwd/env to prod\-db.util
.EE
.SS SEE ALSO
\f[B]tum\-borrow\f[R](1), \f[B]tum\-defs\f[R](5), \f[B]tmux\f[R](1)
